[# Section 1a — The four rings.
   WORKED EXAMPLE for the #826 restructure proposal
   (spec/_archived/docs_restructure_826.md §5). Authored 2026-08-17.

   This section is the guide's own statement of the ring model, which it
   has never had. It is written value-first: a ring is presented as how
   much of CX you take on, and the import contract arrives second as the
   promise that keeps that true — not as the definition.

   Authority: spec/03-approved/core/cx_partition.md §2 (the rings) and §3 (the
   import contracts). The business-facing account stays in
   docs-src/positioning/ring_value_map.md and links here.

   Cross-links use [[anchor]] form. #]

[section id=rings group='Start here' n='1a' title='The four rings'

  [child id=rings-idea n=1a.1 title='How much CX do you have to take on?'
    [intro
      """Most systems make you take all of themselves. You want a config
         format and you get a runtime; you want to parse a file and you
         link a server. CX is built in four **rings**, and a ring answers
         one question: **how much of CX do you have to take on to get the
         thing you want?**"""]

    [table
      [tr [th 'Ring'] [th 'What it is'] [th 'What it cannot do']]
      [tr [td '**0 — Data**'] [td 'the format: values, identity, schema, conversions']
          [td 'execute anything — there is no evaluator in Ring 0']]
      [tr [td '**1 — Code**'] [td 'execution: programs, queries, capabilities']
          [td 'open a socket, start a server, or reach a database']]
      [tr [td '**2 — Platform**'] [td 'the store, history, the wire, services']
          [td 'be embedded — you reach it over a network']]
      [tr [td '**3 — Ecosystem**'] [td 'distribution, registries, the marketplace, bindings']
          [td '(nothing structural — it is the outermost ring)']]]

    [body
      """The order matters in one direction only. A ring may use the rings
         below it and never the ones above. Ring 0 uses nothing of CX at
         all. That single rule is why the small rings stay small: no
         amount of growth in the platform can reach down and make the data
         format heavier."""]

    [note
      """**The boundary people get wrong.** The XAP *host* — the thing that
         runs a feature — is Ring 2. The XAP *marketplace* — the thing you
         discover and install a feature from — is Ring 3. Running is
         platform; distributing is ecosystem."""]]

  [child id=rings-ring0 n=1a.2 title='Ring 0 — data'
    [body
      """Ring 0 is CX as a **data format**. It parses the full grammar,
         gives every value an identity, validates against a schema, and
         converts to and from JSON, XML, YAML, TOML, and Markdown. It has
         no evaluator, and its only dependency is the host language's
         standard library."""]

    [body
      """That last sentence is the product. A Ring-0 consumer can embed CX
         in a place where running someone else's code is not acceptable —
         parsing untrusted input, a build step, a sandboxed client — and
         the guarantee is structural rather than a promise to be careful.
         There is nothing to disable."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[order id=1001 status=:paid
        [line sku='CX-100' qty=2 unit=19.99]
        [line sku='CX-220' qty=1 unit=4.50]]"""]

    [body
      """A **program** is data too. CX is homoiconic, so a Ring-0 consumer
         reads `[?for …]` as an inert element tree the way an XML parser
         reads XSLT — it can hash it, diff it, convert it, and hand it on,
         without any means of running it. Identity therefore belongs to
         Ring 0: a definition hashes to the same bytes everywhere, whether
         or not the reader could execute it."""]

    [body
      """What you can do with only Ring 0: parse and emit; convert between
         formats losslessly; canonicalize; hash, compare, and diff;
         validate against a schema; embed in another language through the
         bindings. See [[data]], [[identity]], [[surfaces]]."""]]

  [child id=rings-ring1 n=1a.3 title='Ring 1 — code'
    [body
      """Ring 1 adds **execution** and nothing else. The evaluator, CXPath,
         the directive set, quasiquotation, purity checking, capability
         enforcement, `fmt`/`lint`, the language server. It adds no
         servers, no protocol stacks, no databases — a Ring-1 deployment
         still cannot open a socket."""]

    [body
      """Effects are deny-by-default. A Ring-1 program that wants to read a
         file, dial a host, or read the clock has to be granted that
         capability on the command line, and a denial names the exact flag
         that would allow it. So \"adds execution\" does not mean \"adds
         reach\": the reach is a separate, explicit decision at every run.
         See [[capabilities]]."""]

    [example lang=sh
      """$ cx report.cx                  # pure: computes, touches nothing
         $ cx --allow-read report.cx     # now it may read files
         $ cx --allow-common report.cx   # the common working set"""]

    [body
      """What you can do with only Ring 1: evaluate programs; query with
         CXPath; transform and generate; run the formatter, linter, and
         language server; call the stdlib packs whose own imports stay
         inside Rings 0–1. See [[code]], [[tour-programs]]."""]]

  [child id=rings-ring2 n=1a.4 title='Ring 2 — platform'
    [body
      """Ring 2 is the **operating platform**: the store and its journal,
         the XSP session layer and its profiles, the HTTP surface, the XAP
         host, the fabric, database drivers, session/authz/identity
         services. This is where data outlives a process and where more
         than one party is involved."""]

    [body
      """Ring 2 is reached **over a network**, not embedded. That is the
         boundary that keeps Rings 0 and 1 small and auditable: the store
         engine itself imports Ring 0 only and stays evaluator-free, so
         running a platform does not drag a server into every program that
         merely reads data."""]

    [body
      """What you can do with only Ring 2: persist and query; keep history
         you can audit and replay; serve HTTP and XSP; host XAP features;
         operate the whole thing. See [[store]], [[operations]]."""]]

  [child id=rings-ring3 n=1a.5 title='Ring 3 — ecosystem'
    [body
      """Ring 3 is everything **around** CX: the registry, the marketplace,
         feature distribution, native clients, and the packaging of the
         language bindings. It is the ring most under construction, and
         this guide says which parts are shipped and which are named."""]

    [body
      """What you can do with only Ring 3: seal, publish, and install
         features; consume packages; reach CX from Go, Python, Rust, or V.
         See [[xap-distribution]], [[bindings]]."""]]

  [child id=rings-contract n=1a.6 title='The import contract'
    [body
      """The rings are not a convention — they are enforced. The contract
         is one rule per ring:"""]

    [example lang=text
      """Ring 0  MUST import nothing internal.
         Ring 1  MAY import Ring 0.
         Ring 2  MAY import Rings 0-1.
         Ring 3  MAY import Rings 0-2."""]

    [body
      """A component may depend on rings *below* its own without stacking
         through the ones between — the shape is a directed graph, not a
         staircase. What no component may ever do is reach upward."""]

    [note
      """This is the promise that makes the earlier sentences true. \"Ring 0
         cannot execute anything\" is not a statement about intent; it is a
         consequence of Ring 0 importing no evaluator, checked by the
         build. \"Ring 1 cannot open a socket\" is the same kind of fact."""]]

  [child id=rings-choosing n=1a.7 title='Where to go from here'
    [body
      """Take the smallest ring that answers your question. You can always
         move outward later — nothing you wrote against a lower ring has to
         change, because the lower ring is unchanged by the higher one.
         [[choosing]] is the router: a task table and four reader paths
         (evaluator, data-format adopter, application author, platform
         operator), each ending in something you can do. If you just want
         to try CX first, start at [[quickstart]] — it runs entirely
         inside Ring 0 before it shows you anything that executes."""]]]
